{
  "abstract": "Kernel-checked proofs, in Lean 4 + Mathlib, of five statements about the factor languages of substitutions. Call a length L oriented when no factor of length L has its reversal among the factors; being oriented passes to every longer length, and fails at L when some factor of length L is a palindrome. The orientation threshold is the least oriented length (one more than the greatest length of a reflected factor). (1) For every n >= 3, the substitution sigma_n on the letters 1, ..., n, i -> i+1 (i < n), n -> 1 2, whose incidence matrix has characteristic polynomial x^n - x - 1, has orientation threshold exactly 4 from the seed 1: some factor of length three is its own reversal (1 2 1), and every factor of length at least four has a reversal that is not a factor. One proof covers every n: the certificate is a set of index rules checked by omega, not an enumeration. n = 3 is the cubic Padovan substitution a -> b, b -> c, c -> ab and n = 4 the quartic substitution a -> b, b -> c, c -> d, d -> ab. (2) For every letter 1 <= x <= n, the factor language of sigma_n from x is the one from 1. (3) The same as (1) for the twelve-letter tensor product of the cubic and the quartic substitution on {p, q, r} x {1, 2, 3, 4}, seed p1 (q1 q2 q1); its incidence matrix is the Kronecker product, with characteristic polynomial the minimal polynomial of rho Q (the product of the two real roots greater than one). The tensor is not a member of the family. (4) The tensor factor language is the same from every seed letter. (5) Statement 3 from every seed and across seeds. No native_decide is used: the family proof is omega and simp, the tensor proof a finite certificate checked by decide / decide +kernel. No physical identification is an axiom, a hypothesis or a conclusion of any statement.",
